      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1098,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\n*   **Composition:** It consists of two parts hydrogen and one part oxygen.\n*   **Bonding:** The atoms are held together by covalent bonds, but the molecule itself is **polar**. This means the oxygen end of the molecule has a slight negative charge, while the hydrogen ends have a slight positive charge. This polarity is what allows water to interact with so many other substances.\n\n### 2. Physical States\nWater is the only common substance on Earth that naturally exists as a solid, liquid, and gas at the temperatures normally found on the planet:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, water molecules form a rigid, crystalline structure.\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state we are most familiar with. It flows and takes the shape of its container.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When heated, water molecules move so fast they escape into the air as a gas.\n\n### 3. Unique Properties\nWater has several properties that make it essential for life and the planet:\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Because of its polarity, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This allows it to transport nutrients and minerals within living organisms and move them through the environment.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together strongly at the surface, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This allows insects to walk on water and helps plants pull water up from their roots against gravity.\n*   **High Specific Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat without getting hot. This helps regulate the Earth's climate and stabilizes body temperatures in living things.\n\n### 4. Biological Importance\nWater is the foundation of life as we know it. It is a major component of all known living organisms (making up about 60% of the human body). It is used for:\n*   Chemical reactions in cells.\n*   Transporting nutrients and waste.\n*   Lubricating joints and organs.\n*   Regulating body temperature through sweating and panting."
